# Expectation record for 07_variadics/002_mixed_int_double_promotions.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 002_mixed_int_double_promotions
area               = 07_variadics
description        = Default argument promotions for float and the narrow integer types
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Arguments are supplied in the unpromoted types and retrieved in the promoted ones, so
va_arg always names the type actually present in the list: naming the type written at the
call site would be undefined behaviour, and a divergence on an undefined program would
prove nothing about either compiler.  No list is read past the argument count and each is
ended exactly once.  Every floating literal is a dyadic rational exactly representable in
IEEE binary32 and binary64, so the printed text is exact.  <stdarg.h> is included because a
variadic function cannot be written without it and it is in both compilers' freestanding
sets; printf is still hand-declared, because bcc ships no stdio.h.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
fflt[0]=1.500000
fflt[1]=2.250000
fflt[2]=4.125000
fflt_sum=7.875000
rflt[0]=0.250000
